WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.197
This lesson fixes one of the easiest graph mistakes to make.

2
00:00:04.197 --> 00:00:08.393
A learner sees a slope and decides immediately what it means, without checking the axes.

3
00:00:08.393 --> 00:00:12.590
But slope does not carry a permanent physics meaning by itself.

4
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.787
The axes decide.

5
00:00:16.787 --> 00:00:20.983
On a distance-time graph, slope means speed.

6
00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:25.180
On a speed-time graph, slope means acceleration.

7
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.377
So the same visual steepness can answer different physics questions once the graph type changes.

8
00:00:29.377 --> 00:00:33.573
Keep this takeaway in view: Slope meaning comes from the axes, not from steepness alone.

9
00:00:33.573 --> 00:00:37.770
Same geometry means same tilt.

10
00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.967
The shape on the page can look very similar.

11
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:46.163
Different axes means different meaning.

12
00:00:46.163 --> 00:00:50.360
The labels decide what the slope is measuring.

13
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.734
On the distance-time graph, the slope is change in distance divided by change in time.

14
00:00:55.734 --> 00:01:01.108
That gives metres per second, which is speed or pace.

15
00:01:01.108 --> 00:01:06.481
So when the distance-time line is steeper, the object is adding more distance each second.

16
00:01:06.481 --> 00:01:11.855
The graph is not doing anything mysterious.

17
00:01:11.855 --> 00:01:17.229
The units are telling the story: metres over seconds becomes metres per second.

18
00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:22.602
Keep this takeaway in view: Distance over time gives speed, so distance-time slope belongs to pace.

19
00:01:22.602 --> 00:01:27.976
Vertical axis: Distance is being measured upward.

20
00:01:27.976 --> 00:01:33.350
Horizontal axis: Time is measured left to right.

21
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:39.529
Now switch to the speed-time graph.

22
00:01:39.529 --> 00:01:45.708
The geometry of slope still means rise over run, but the rise is now change in speed rather than change in distance.

23
00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:51.886
So the slope becomes change in speed divided by time, which is acceleration.

24
00:01:51.886 --> 00:01:58.065
That is why the same steepness that once named a speed can now name an acceleration.

25
00:01:58.065 --> 00:02:04.244
The graph type, not the visual tilt alone, makes the difference.

26
00:02:04.244 --> 00:02:10.422
Keep this takeaway in view: Changing the vertical axis changes the meaning of the same geometric slope.

27
00:02:10.422 --> 00:02:16.601
Vertical axis: Speed is now being measured upward.

28
00:02:16.601 --> 00:02:22.780
Horizontal axis: Time is still measured left to right.

29
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.648
A useful analogy is the same tilt shown on two screens.

30
00:02:27.648 --> 00:02:32.516
On one screen, the scoreboard labels the vertical axis as distance.

31
00:02:32.516 --> 00:02:37.385
On the other, it labels the vertical axis as speed.

32
00:02:37.385 --> 00:02:42.253
The gesture of the line can look the same, but the meaning changes because the scoreboard is measuring something different.

33
00:02:42.253 --> 00:02:47.121
This analogy helps because it shows that the physics meaning is not hidden in the line alone.

34
00:02:47.121 --> 00:02:51.989
It comes from the line plus the labels.

35
00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:56.857
Keep this takeaway in view: Line shape without axis meaning is not enough for correct interpretation.

36
00:02:56.857 --> 00:03:01.725
Screen one means distance scoreboard.

37
00:03:01.725 --> 00:03:06.594
The tilt is interpreted as speed.

38
00:03:06.594 --> 00:03:11.462
Screen two means speed scoreboard.

39
00:03:11.462 --> 00:03:16.330
The same tilt is interpreted as acceleration.

40
00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:21.284
A zero gradient shows the same context rule.

41
00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:26.238
On a distance-time graph, zero slope means distance is not changing, so the object is stopped.

42
00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:31.192
On a speed-time graph, zero slope means the speed is not changing, which can describe steady motion or rest depending on the graph height.

43
00:03:31.192 --> 00:03:36.146
So even a flat line needs the axes to be interpreted correctly.

44
00:03:36.146 --> 00:03:41.100
That is why this lesson keeps repeating context first.

45
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:46.054
Keep this takeaway in view: Even zero slope needs graph context before it can be interpreted correctly.

46
00:03:46.054 --> 00:03:51.008
Distance-time flat means stopped.

47
00:03:51.008 --> 00:03:55.962
No change in distance means no motion.

48
00:03:55.962 --> 00:04:00.916
Speed-time flat means constant speed.

49
00:04:00.916 --> 00:04:05.870
The speed does not change, but it may still be above zero.

50
00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:10.577
A common trap is to memorize one sentence such as slope means speed and then apply it everywhere.

51
00:04:10.577 --> 00:04:15.283
That works only for one graph type.

52
00:04:15.283 --> 00:04:19.990
Another trap is to ignore the units that come from the axes.

53
00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:24.697
Metres over seconds gives metres per second.

54
00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:29.403
Metres per second over seconds gives metres per second squared.

55
00:04:29.403 --> 00:04:34.110
The units themselves reveal the physics meaning if the learner pays attention to the labels.

56
00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:38.817
Keep this takeaway in view: Slope meaning is not portable between graph types unless the axes are checked again.

57
00:04:38.817 --> 00:04:43.523
Name the graph type first.

58
00:04:43.523 --> 00:04:48.230
Use the axes to decide what the rise and run are measuring.

59
00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:51.200
Ask yourself three questions.

60
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.170
On a distance-time graph, what does the slope mean?

61
00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:57.140
Speed.

62
00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:00.110
On a speed-time graph, what does the slope mean?

63
00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.080
Acceleration.

64
00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:06.050
And if two graphs have the same tilt but different vertical axes, what must change?

65
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.020
The physics meaning and the units.

66
00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.990
Those three answers are the complete logic of the lesson.

67
00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:14.960
Keep this takeaway in view: The graph type comes first; the slope meaning follows from it.

68
00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.930
Distance-time slope means speed.

69
00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.900
Distance change divided by time change.

70
00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.870
Speed-time slope means acceleration.

71
00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.840
Speed change divided by time change.

72
00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:33.131
You are ready for the worked example now.

73
00:05:33.131 --> 00:05:39.422
The distance-time graph rises from four metres to sixteen metres in six seconds.

74
00:05:39.422 --> 00:05:45.712
Its gradient is twelve divided by six, which is two metres per second, so that slope means speed.

75
00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:52.003
The speed-time graph rises from three metres per second to nine metres per second in three seconds.

76
00:05:52.003 --> 00:05:58.294
Its gradient is six divided by three, which is two metres per second squared, so that slope means acceleration.

77
00:05:58.294 --> 00:06:04.585
The same number appears, but the axes change the quantity and the unit.

78
00:06:04.585 --> 00:06:10.876
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: slope meaning follows the axes every time.

79
00:06:10.876 --> 00:06:17.167
Step 1: Name the graph type before calculating the slope.

80
00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:23.458
Step 2: Calculate the slope using the correct vertical quantity for that graph.

81
00:06:23.458 --> 00:06:29.748
distance-time: (16 - 4) / 6 = 2 m/s.

82
00:06:29.748 --> 00:06:36.039
speed-time: (9 - 3) / 3 = 2 m/s^2.

83
00:06:36.039 --> 00:06:42.330
The number can match while the physics meaning still changes with the axes.
